#!/usr/bin/env python3
# section: 1
"""hee-scrob -- terse, punk-as-fuck now-playing scrobble, real Plex source.

Real trigger (2026-08-22): "terse and hee and very spencer for when I
/scrob in #tclug" -- fleet-ops#129's long-standing scrobbler gap
finally getting built. Real Plex server: plex.crooked.tcos.us, docker
on nuc-1. Then extended, same conversation: "song/show year, ts of
point at scrobble and text (thousands of .srt files in media/) and
cool short description and punk as fuck" -- for video content, pulls
the real subtitle line active at the exact playback position from the
real .srt sitting next to the media file. No invented dialogue --
if there's no real .srt, there's no subtitle line in the output.

Real fix (2026-08-22, same session): TV episodes were only showing the
episode's own title ("LASIK Instinct"), silently dropping the real
show name and season/episode -- Plex's `title` attribute on an
episode Video element is the *episode* title, not the show's; the show
name lives in `grandparentTitle`, season in `parentIndex`, episode
number in `index`. Real ask: "needs the TV show, s N e N year device
friendly name." Device names got the same real fix -- reverse-DNS
mostly fails on this LAN (no PTR records), so Plex's raw self-reported
device title ("LG 32LM570BPUA") was showing instead of a real human
name. No API to pull that mapping live (Home Assistant's reachable but
needs a token nobody has yet) -- so a real, local override file
instead of fabricating one: ~/.config/hee/device-names.txt, one
"raw-name = Friendly Name" pair per line, checked before falling back
to whatever Plex/reverse-DNS gave us.

Designed to be wired as an irssi alias that pipes stdout into a channel:
  SCROB = "EXEC -o ~/.local/bin/hee-scrob"
then just `/scrob` in the channel.

Auth: never hardcoded, never printed. This tool opens its own sealed
credential, so the plain invocation is the whole thing:
  hee scrob
The token is resolved in this order, and the first hit wins:
  1. $PLEX_TOKEN -- what `hee cred -run plex-token -exec` injects.
  2. the sealed `plex-token` credential, opened by this tool via
     hee-cred (HEE#279) against $HEE_CRED_DIR, defaulting to the HOME
     store ~/.hee/secrets.
  3. ~/.config/hee/plex-token, a real file for contexts where gpg
     cannot run at all.
$HEE_CRED_PASS is NOT read as the token. hee-cred injects every sealed
credential under that one name and this tool needs two -- the Discord
invocation sets it to the WEBHOOK URL, which the old code then sent to
Plex as an X-Plex-Token header. -discord still reads it, where it is
unambiguous by construction.
Step 2 exists because requiring a wrapper put two things on the caller
that are easy to get silently wrong: WHICH store (hee-cred's default is
relative to the current directory, so a wrapper without an explicit one
works from ~ and dies one `cd` later) and THAT A WRAPPER EXISTS (a
bash alias is invisible to cron, to `ssh host cmd`, to CI, and to
irssi's EXEC -- which is this tool's whole point). hee-scrob already
knows which account it needs; holding that here is the fix.

Which player: a Plex server reports EVERY session on it, so on a shared
server "what is playing" is a question, not a fact. This tool used to
answer it with whichever session came first, which is how the operator
watching on flippy got told about someone else's show. Claim your players
once and it reports yours:

  hee-scrob -players              # seen now + known to the server, claimed ones marked
  hee-scrob -claim flippy         # writes `claim = flippy` in ~/.config/hee/scrob.rc
  hee-scrob -player flippy        # this once, whatever the rc says

A claim matches a player's id, the title Plex reports, or its friendly
name from device-names.txt. Two players can share a title (a Plexamp and
a Plex for Linux both called "flippy"): claiming the title takes both,
claiming the id takes exactly one. With claims set and none of them
playing, this says so and reports nothing -- silence beats scrobbling a
stranger's show. With no claims at all it reports whatever is playing and
says how to make the choice yours.

Usage:
  hee-scrob [-server URL]
  hee-scrob -players | -claim NAME | -unclaim NAME | -player NAME
  hee-scrob -discord   # post the same real line to Discord instead of stdout

Discord posting (real trigger, 2026-08-29, Spencer direct: "yes hee
scrob should do this"): reads the webhook URL from $HEE_CRED_PASS,
the same real env var hee-cred -exec injects -- never a hardcoded
URL, never printed. Real invocation:
  hee cred -pass discord-webhook-scrob -exec hee-scrob -discord
Posts the exact same text -discord would otherwise print to stdout --
one real formatting path, not two that can drift. Skips posting (does
not spam the channel) when nothing is actually playing.
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import urllib.request
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
from pathlib import Path

# library/py lives in this tool's own repo -- derive it from this script's
# own location, resolved through symlinks, never from the repo the tool
# happens to be invoked against.
from pathlib import Path as _Path  # noqa: E402
sys.path.insert(0, str(_Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent / "library" / "py"))
from hee_cli import help_wanted, note_ignored  # noqa: E402

DEFAULT_SERVER = "https://plex.crooked.tcos.us"
DEFAULT_TOKEN_FILE = Path.home() / ".config" / "hee" / "plex-token"
# The sealed accounts this tool needs, held here so no caller has to know
# them. Two DIFFERENT credentials, which is the whole reason $HEE_CRED_PASS
# can no longer be trusted to hold either one -- see read_token().
CRED_ACCOUNT = "plex-token"
DISCORD_ACCOUNT = "discord-webhook-scrob"
DEVICE_NAMES_FILE = Path.home() / ".config" / "hee" / "device-names.txt"
# The rc file: which players on this Plex are MINE. Operator, 2026-09-20:
# "currently the app only seems to take the first our last player it sees
# (ie I am playing on flippy but hee scrob shows the media from another
# player on the network). should be able to scan the seen and/or known
# network clients and 'claim' the ones you want to scrob. maybe an rc file
# for this". One `claim = ` line per player, matched against the player's
# machineIdentifier, its title, or its device-names.txt friendly name.
RC_FILE = Path.home() / ".config" / "hee" / "scrob.rc"
RC_HEADER = """# hee-scrob rc -- which players on this Plex server are yours.
# One `claim =` per player: its id, its title, or its friendly name from
# device-names.txt. `hee scrob -players` lists what is seen and known;
# `hee scrob -claim NAME` adds a line here. With no claims at all, scrob
# takes whatever is playing, which on a shared server is a coin toss.
# `server =` overrides the default Plex; the -server flag beats both.
"""
# HEE#670: Plex's `file` attribute is a path as the CONTAINER sees it, not
# this host. Per-oper in heerc, e.g. HEE_SCROB_PATH_MAP="/data=/srv/media".
PATH_MAP_VAR = "HEE_SCROB_PATH_MAP"


def unseal(account: str) -> str:
    """Open the sealed `plex-token` credential ourselves.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. The documented invocation used to be a wrapper the
    caller had to supply:

        hee cred -run plex-token -exec hee scrob

    That is correct and it kept failing, three times in one session, in
    three different ways -- because it pushes two pieces of knowledge onto
    every caller, and both are easy to get wrong silently:

      1. WHICH STORE. `hee cred`'s default store is `.hee/secrets`
         RELATIVE TO THE CURRENT DIRECTORY. A wrapper without an explicit
         store works from ~ and dies one `cd` later:
             ~ $ plexrun hee scrob                       -> np: ...
             ~/git/fleet-ops/view $ plexrun hee scrob
             hee-cred: no sealed credential at
               .../git/fleet-ops/view/.hee/secrets/plex-token.gpg
         Nothing broke. The relative default is deliberate -- a repo
         carries its own store -- which is exactly why a HOME-store
         credential has to name HEE_CRED_DIR, and why a hand-rolled
         wrapper is the wrong place to remember that.

      2. THAT A WRAPPER EXISTS AT ALL. `plexrun` lives in
         ~/.bash_aliases, and an alias exists ONLY in an interactive
         bash. It is invisible to cron, to `ssh host cmd`, to CI, and --
         the case that matters most here -- to irssi's EXEC, which is the
         whole point of this tool. Measured 2026-09-10: on two hosts the
         wrapper did not exist at all, because their ~/.bashrc predates
         both the heerc source line and the alias-file block.

    hee-scrob already knows the account name it needs. Asking the caller
    to supply it, from the right directory, in a shell that happens to be
    wired for it, is knowledge this tool should hold itself.

    Never printed and never written to disk -- the value crosses one pipe
    into this process and stays here.
    """
    # SIBLING FIRST, and that is not a micro-optimization. hee-scrob IS a
    # hee tool, so hee-cred is by construction the file next to it -- while
    # PATH is a property of the calling shell, and the calling shell here is
    # irssi's EXEC, or cron, or a non-login `ssh host cmd`, none of which
    # source a profile. Measured 2026-09-10: this function returned nothing
    # on a host where the credential opened perfectly by hand, purely
    # because neither `hee-cred` nor `hee` was on PATH in that context.
    # Resolving next to __file__ needs no PATH and bakes in no absolute
    # path, so it holds wherever the checkout lives.
    sibling = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "hee-cred"
    if sibling.is_file() and os.access(sibling, os.X_OK):
        argv = [str(sibling)]
    elif shutil.which("hee-cred"):
        argv = ["hee-cred"]
    elif shutil.which("hee"):
        argv = ["hee", "cred"]
    else:
        return ""
    argv += ["-pass", account, "-exec", "sh", "-c", 'printf %s "$HEE_CRED_PASS"']

    env = dict(os.environ)
    # The store is named EXPLICITLY and absolutely. Falling through to the
    # cwd-relative default is the bug this function exists to remove, so it
    # is not an option here -- an operator who has pinned HEE_CRED_DIR
    # deliberately still wins.
    env.setdefault("HEE_CRED_DIR", str(Path.home() / ".hee" / "secrets"))

    try:
        # A timeout, because gpg can block on an agent prompt and this runs
        # from irssi's EXEC where there is no terminal to prompt on. Better
        # to fall through to the token file than to hang a client.
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15, env=env)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""


def read_token() -> str:
    """Resolve the token, cheapest and most explicit first.

    1. $PLEX_TOKEN -- what `hee cred -run plex-token -exec` injects, and
       unambiguous because it is named for this credential.
    2. the sealed `plex-token` credential, opened by this tool (see unseal).
    3. ~/.config/hee/plex-token -- the real escape hatch for a context
       where gpg cannot run at all. irssi's EXEC inherits only the env
       vars present when irssi itself launched and does not source
       .profile, so a PLEX_TOKEN exported afterwards never reaches /SCROB
       without a full session restart. A file needs no agent, no tty and
       no restart.

    $HEE_CRED_PASS IS DELIBERATELY NOT READ HERE ANY MORE, and dropping it
    fixes a real collision rather than tidying up. hee-cred injects EVERY
    sealed credential under that one fixed name, and this tool needs TWO
    different ones. The documented Discord invocation is

        hee cred -pass discord-webhook-scrob -exec hee-scrob -discord

    which sets $HEE_CRED_PASS to the WEBHOOK URL. The old code read that
    same variable as the Plex token, so `-discord` sent the webhook URL to
    the Plex server in an X-Plex-Token header -- leaking one credential to
    a third party and failing the scrobble with 401 on the way.

    Nothing regresses by removing it: the previously documented
    `hee cred -pass plex-token -exec hee scrob` still works, because step 2
    now opens that same credential without being told to.
    """
    env_token = os.environ.get("PLEX_TOKEN")
    if env_token:
        return env_token
    sealed = unseal(CRED_ACCOUNT)
    if sealed:
        return sealed
    if DEFAULT_TOKEN_FILE.exists():
        return DEFAULT_TOKEN_FILE.read_text().strip()
    return ""


def read_device_names() -> dict:
    """Real, local, human-maintained override -- 'raw = Friendly' per
    line, '#' comments allowed. Nothing invented if a raw name isn't
    in here; the raw value just passes through unchanged."""
    mapping = {}
    if not DEVICE_NAMES_FILE.exists():
        return mapping
    for line in DEVICE_NAMES_FILE.read_text().splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
            continue
        raw, friendly = line.split("=", 1)
        mapping[raw.strip()] = friendly.strip()
    return mapping


def read_rc(path=None) -> dict:
    """The rc file as {"claims": [str], "server": str|None}. Same shape as
    device-names.txt (key = value, '#' comments), because a second syntax
    for a second file in the same directory is a second thing to remember."""
    rc = {"claims": [], "server": None}
    path = Path(path) if path else RC_FILE
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return rc
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
        if not line or "=" not in line:
            continue
        key, value = (x.strip() for x in line.split("=", 1))
        if not value:
            continue
        if key == "claim":
            rc["claims"].append(value)
        elif key == "server":
            rc["server"] = value
    return rc


def write_claim(name: str, remove: bool = False, path=None) -> str:
    """Add or remove one `claim =` line, keeping every other line as it is.
    Returns what happened, in the tool's own words."""
    path = Path(path) if path else RC_FILE
    try:
        lines = path.read_text().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        lines = []
    def is_claim(line, value):
        bare = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
        return "=" in bare and [x.strip() for x in bare.split("=", 1)] == ["claim", value]
    if remove:
        kept = [ln for ln in lines if not is_claim(ln, name)]
        if len(kept) == len(lines):
            return f"hee-scrob: {name} was not claimed in {path}"
        lines = kept
        what = f"hee-scrob: unclaimed {name} in {path}"
    else:
        if any(is_claim(ln, name) for ln in lines):
            return f"hee-scrob: {name} is already claimed in {path}"
        if not lines:
            lines = RC_HEADER.rstrip("\n").splitlines()
        lines.append(f"claim = {name}")
        what = f"hee-scrob: claimed {name} in {path}"
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path.write_text("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    return what


def player_of(el, device_names):
    """One session's player as data: its stable id, the title Plex reports,
    the friendly name device-names.txt gives it, product and state."""
    p = el.find("Player") if el is not None else None
    if p is None:
        return {}
    title = p.get("title") or ""
    return {"id": p.get("machineIdentifier") or "", "title": title,
            "name": device_names.get(title, title), "product": p.get("product") or p.get("platform") or "",
            "address": p.get("address") or "", "state": (p.get("state") or "").lower(),
            "user": (el.find("User").get("title") if el.find("User") is not None else "") or ""}


def claimed(player: dict, claims) -> bool:
    """Does this player answer to any claim? A claim matches its id, the
    title Plex reports, or its device-names.txt friendly name -- case and
    surrounding space ignored. Two players can share a title (a Plexamp
    and a Plex for Linux both called 'flippy'), so a claim by title takes
    both on purpose; claim the id to take exactly one."""
    if not claims:
        return False
    fields = {str(player.get(k) or "").strip().lower() for k in ("id", "title", "name")}
    fields.discard("")
    return any(str(c).strip().lower() in fields for c in claims)


def choose_session(sessions, claims, want=None):
    """The session to report, and why -- (element, player, note).

    Plex returns every session on the server in its own order, so
    `find("Video")` reported whoever happened to be first: the operator
    watching on flippy saw someone else's show. Order here is deliberate:
    a -player argument narrows first, then the rc's claims, and among what
    is left a player actually PLAYING beats one that is paused."""
    pairs = [(el, p) for el, p in sessions]
    note = None
    if want:
        narrowed = [(el, p) for el, p in pairs if claimed(p, [want])]
        if not narrowed:
            seen = ", ".join(sorted({p["name"] or p["id"] for _, p in pairs})) or "nothing"
            return None, None, f"hee-scrob: no session on a player matching {want!r} (playing now: {seen})"
        pairs = narrowed
    elif claims:
        narrowed = [(el, p) for el, p in pairs if claimed(p, claims)]
        if not narrowed and pairs:
            seen = ", ".join(sorted({f"{p['name']} [{p['id']}]" for _, p in pairs}))
            return None, None, (f"hee-scrob: {len(pairs)} session(s) playing, none on a claimed player: {seen}\n"
                                f"hee-scrob: `hee scrob -players` lists them; `hee scrob -claim NAME` claims one")
        pairs = narrowed
    elif len(pairs) > 1:
        note = (f"hee-scrob: {len(pairs)} players are playing and none is claimed -- reporting the one that is playing.\n"
                f"hee-scrob: `hee scrob -players` lists them; `hee scrob -claim NAME` makes the choice yours ({RC_FILE})")
    if not pairs:
        return None, None, note
    pairs.sort(key=lambda ep: 0 if ep[1].get("state") == "playing" else 1)
    return pairs[0][0], pairs[0][1], note


def list_players(server, token, device_names, claims, fetch=None):
    """Every player: the ones with a session right now, then the ones this
    server knows from the account's devices. Claimed ones are marked. The
    id is what a claim should use when two players share a title."""
    fetch = fetch or (lambda path: _get_xml(server, token, path))
    rows, seen_ids = [], set()
    root = fetch("/status/sessions")
    for el in list(root.findall("Video")) + list(root.findall("Track")):
        p = player_of(el, device_names)
        if not p:
            continue
        seen_ids.add(p["id"])
        rows.append({**p, "where": "playing now", "claimed": claimed(p, claims)})
    known = []
    try:
        for d in fetch("/devices").findall("Device"):
            cid = d.get("clientIdentifier") or ""
            if not cid or cid in seen_ids:
                continue
            title = d.get("name") or ""
            p = {"id": cid, "title": title, "name": device_names.get(title, title),
                 "product": d.get("platform") or "", "address": "", "state": "", "user": ""}
            known.append({**p, "where": "known", "claimed": claimed(p, claims),
                          "created": int(d.get("createdAt") or 0)})
    except (ET.ParseError, OSError, ValueError):
        pass   # the account's device list is a convenience; the live sessions are the point
    known.sort(key=lambda r: -r["created"])
    claimed_known = [r for r in known if r["claimed"]]
    rest = [r for r in known if not r["claimed"]]
    return rows, claimed_known + rest


def format_players(playing, known, claims, limit=20):
    out = []

    def mark(r):
        return "*" if r["claimed"] else " "

    out.append("PLAYING NOW" if playing else "PLAYING NOW: nothing")
    for r in playing:
        out.append(f" {mark(r)} {r['name'] or '(no name)'}  [{r['id']}]  {r['product']}  {r['state']}"
                   + (f"  user {r['user']}" if r["user"] else "") + (f"  {r['address']}" if r["address"] else ""))
    shown = known[:limit]
    out.append(f"KNOWN TO THIS SERVER ({len(known)} device(s)" + (f", {limit} shown)" if len(known) > limit else ")"))
    for r in shown:
        out.append(f" {mark(r)} {r['name'] or '(no name)'}  [{r['id']}]  {r['product']}")
    out.append("")
    out.append(f"* = claimed in {RC_FILE}" if claims else f"nothing claimed yet ({RC_FILE} does not list a claim)")
    out.append("claim one by name or by id:  hee scrob -claim NAME   (hee scrob -unclaim NAME to drop it)")
    if not claims:
        out.append("with no claims scrob reports whichever player is playing, which on a shared server is a coin toss")
    return "\n".join(out)


def fmt_ts(ms: int) -> str:
    s = max(ms, 0) // 1000
    h, s = divmod(s, 3600)
    m, s = divmod(s, 60)
    return f"{h:02d}:{m:02d}:{s:02d}"


def _srt_dialogue(block: str, ts_re) -> str:
    lines = block.strip().splitlines()
    dialogue = [l for l in lines if l.strip() and not ts_re.search(l) and not l.strip().isdigit()]
    return " ".join(dialogue).strip()


def _srt_blocks(srt_path: Path):
    text = srt_path.read_text(errors="replace")
    ts_re = re.compile(
        r"(\d{2}):(\d{2}):(\d{2}),(\d{3})\s*-->\s*(\d{2}):(\d{2}):(\d{2}),(\d{3})"
    )
    parsed = []
    for block in text.split("\n\n"):
        m = ts_re.search(block)
        if not m:
            continue
        h1, m1, s1, ms1, h2, m2, s2, ms2 = map(int, m.groups())
        start = ((h1 * 3600 + m1 * 60 + s1) * 1000) + ms1
        end = ((h2 * 3600 + m2 * 60 + s2) * 1000) + ms2
        parsed.append((start, end, _srt_dialogue(block, ts_re)))
    return parsed, ts_re


def srt_line_at_offset(blocks, offset_ms: int):
    """Real, discrete lookup at one exact moment -- returns the real
    dialogue active there, or None. No fabricated line when the moment
    genuinely falls in a silent gap between subtitle blocks -- an
    empty result there is correct, not a bug."""
    for start, end, text in blocks:
        if start <= offset_ms <= end:
            return text or None
    return None


def srt_context(srt_path: Path, offset_ms: int, current_only: bool = False):
    """Real trigger: "need 00:00:00 for scrob and for tv/movie 5s
    before, cur, 5s after, discrete things" -- three independent,
    fixed-offset lookups (offset-5000, offset, offset+5000), not
    whatever subtitle block happens to be structurally adjacent. Each
    one is real and separately correct if empty.

    Real extension (2026-08-22): "now a switch for the current sub
    titles" -- -current-only drops the before/after context and
    returns just the exact-moment line, still real, still None if
    that moment falls in a silent gap."""
    if not srt_path.exists():
        return None
    blocks, _ = _srt_blocks(srt_path)
    if current_only:
        return srt_line_at_offset(blocks, offset_ms)
    before = srt_line_at_offset(blocks, offset_ms - 5000)
    current = srt_line_at_offset(blocks, offset_ms)
    after = srt_line_at_offset(blocks, offset_ms + 5000)
    parts = [p for p in (before, current, after) if p]
    return " / ".join(parts) if parts else None


def find_srt(media_file: str):
    p = Path(media_file)
    for candidate in [p.with_suffix(".en.srt"), p.with_suffix(".srt")]:
        if candidate.exists():
            return candidate
    return None


def parse_path_map(raw: str):
    """Parse HEE_SCROB_PATH_MAP ("/data=/srv/media,/tv=/srv/tv") into
    (container, local) pairs, longest container prefix first so the most
    specific mapping always wins in map_local_path. A pair missing '=' or
    with either side empty is skipped -- one malformed pair should not
    break the rest of the map."""
    pairs = []
    for chunk in (raw or "").split(","):
        chunk = chunk.strip()
        if not chunk or "=" not in chunk:
            continue
        container, _, local = chunk.partition("=")
        container = container.strip().rstrip("/")
        local = local.strip().rstrip("/")
        if not container or not local:
            continue
        pairs.append((container, local))
    pairs.sort(key=lambda pair: len(pair[0]), reverse=True)
    return pairs


def map_local_path(path: str, mappings):
    """Rewrite the leading container-path component of `path` using the
    longest-matching prefix in `mappings`. A prefix matches only on a
    path-component boundary -- '/data' must not match '/database'."""
    for container, local in mappings:
        if path == container or path.startswith(container + "/"):
            return local + path[len(container):]
    return path


def resolve_media_file(media_file: str, mappings):
    """Apply the path map and report whether the result exists on this
    filesystem. No Plex involved -- a test calls this with a bare string
    and a temp dir."""
    local_file = map_local_path(media_file, mappings)
    return local_file, Path(local_file).exists()


def warn_unresolved(media_file: str, mappings):
    """HEE#670's distinguishable message: an unmapped/unresolvable path
    must never look like outcome (2), a genuinely missing sidecar .srt.
    Always stderr -- stdout is piped straight into an IRC/Discord channel."""
    tried = ", ".join(f"{c}={l}" for c, l in mappings) if mappings else "none configured"
    print(
        f"hee-scrob: media path not found locally: {media_file}\n"
        f"hee-scrob: {PATH_MAP_VAR} mappings tried: {tried}",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )


def _get_xml(server: str, token: str, path: str):
    sep = "&" if "?" in path else "?"
    req = urllib.request.Request(f"{server}{path}{sep}X-Plex-Token={token}", headers={"Accept": "application/xml"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=10) as r:
        return ET.fromstring(r.read())


def now_playing(server: str, token: str, device_names: dict, current_only: bool = False,
                claims=None, want=None, say=None):
    root = _get_xml(server, token, "/status/sessions")
    sessions = [(el, player_of(el, device_names)) for el in (list(root.findall("Video")) + list(root.findall("Track")))]
    sessions = [(el, p) for el, p in sessions if p]
    chosen, _player, note = choose_session(sessions, claims or [], want)
    if note:
        (say or (lambda m: print(m, file=sys.stderr)))(note)
    if chosen is None:
        return None
    video = chosen if chosen.tag == "Video" else None
    track = chosen if chosen.tag == "Track" else None

    def device_name(el):
        """Real hostname of the playing device, not just Plex's
        self-reported friendly title -- reverse-DNS the Player's real
        connecting IP first, fall back to the title Plex gives us if
        that fails (most devices on this network won't have PTR
        records, same real gap as everything else tonight). Then check
        the local device-names.txt override for a real human-assigned
        friendly name over whatever raw value came out of either path."""
        p = el.find("Player")
        if p is None:
            return None
        raw = None
        address = p.get("address")
        if address:
            try:
                raw = socket.gethostbyaddr(address)[0]
            except (socket.herror, socket.gaierror):
                pass
        if not raw:
            raw = p.get("title")
        if raw and raw in device_names:
            return device_names[raw]
        return raw

    if video is not None:
        year = video.get("year") or video.get("parentYear") or ""
        offset = int(video.get("viewOffset") or 0)
        part = video.find("./Media/Part")
        srt_text = None
        local_file = None
        if part is not None:
            media_file = part.get("file")
            if media_file:
                mappings = parse_path_map(os.environ.get(PATH_MAP_VAR, ""))
                local_file, exists = resolve_media_file(media_file, mappings)
                if not exists:
                    local_file = None
                if exists:
                    srt_path = find_srt(local_file)
                    if srt_path:
                        srt_text = srt_context(srt_path, offset, current_only)
                else:
                    warn_unresolved(media_file, mappings)

        if video.get("type") == "episode":
            show = video.get("grandparentTitle") or "?"
            season = video.get("parentIndex")
            episode = video.get("index")
            se = f"s{int(season):02d}e{int(episode):02d}" if season and episode else ""
            ep_title = video.get("title") or ""
            display = f"{show} {se} - {ep_title}".strip() if ep_title else f"{show} {se}".strip()
            return {"kind": "video", "title": display, "year": year, "ts": fmt_ts(offset), "line": srt_text, "device": device_name(video),
                    "file": local_file, "offset_ms": offset}

        title = video.get("title") or "?"
        return {"kind": "video", "title": title, "year": year, "ts": fmt_ts(offset), "line": srt_text, "device": device_name(video),
                    "file": local_file, "offset_ms": offset}

    if track is not None:
        artist = track.get("grandparentTitle") or track.get("originalTitle") or "?"
        title = track.get("title") or "?"
        album = track.get("parentTitle") or ""
        year = track.get("parentYear") or track.get("year") or ""
        offset = int(track.get("viewOffset") or 0)
        return {"kind": "track", "artist": artist, "title": title, "album": album, "year": year, "ts": fmt_ts(offset), "device": device_name(track)}

    return None


def format_line(r):
    """Build the real np: line from a now_playing() result -- shared by
    stdout printing and Discord posting, one real formatting path."""
    dev = f" ({r['device']})" if r.get("device") else ""
    if r["kind"] == "track":
        yr = f" ({r['year']})" if r["year"] else ""
        alb = f" [{r['album']}]" if r["album"] else ""
        return f"np: {r['artist']} - {r['title']}{yr}{alb} @ {r['ts']}{dev}"
    yr = f" ({r['year']})" if r["year"] else ""
    line = f' -- {r["line"]}' if r["line"] else ""
    return f"np: {r['title']}{yr} @ {r['ts']}{line}{dev}"


def to_meme(r, name, hee=None):
    """Hand what is playing to `hee meme quote --file --at --job`: the local
    file and the moment. hee meme looks the cue up itself (the sidecar,
    else the episode's own stream) and writes the job; the line scrob saw
    rides along as the caption for a silent moment. Operator, 2026-09-19:
    "pipe from hee scrob to hee meme ... hee scrob -to meme would be ideal,
    so oper doesn't even need a pipe". Returns the exit code of hee meme,
    whose own words are the output."""
    if r.get("kind") != "video":
        print("hee-scrob: -to meme wants a video; what is playing is a track", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not r.get("file"):
        print(f"hee-scrob: -to meme needs the file locally and it is not here ({PATH_MAP_VAR}?)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    hee = hee or shutil.which("hee") or str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "hee")
    at = f"{fmt_ts(r['offset_ms'])}.{r['offset_ms'] % 1000:03d}"   # the exact moment, millis kept
    cmd = [hee, "meme", "quote", "--file", r["file"], "--at", at, "--job", name]
    line = (r.get("line") or "").strip()
    if line:
        cmd += ["--caption", line]
    print(f"hee-scrob: {r['title']} @ {r['ts']} -> hee meme quote --file ... --at {at} --job {name}")
    return subprocess.call(cmd)


def post_discord(text):
    # $HEE_CRED_PASS is still honored here, and here it is unambiguous:
    # the documented Discord invocation wraps exactly this credential, so
    # whatever hee-cred injected is the webhook by construction. If nothing
    # wrapped us, open the sealed account ourselves -- same as the token, so
    # `hee scrob -discord` needs no wrapper either.
    webhook_url = os.environ.get("HEE_CRED_PASS") or unseal(DISCORD_ACCOUNT)
    if not webhook_url:
        sys.exit(
            f"hee-scrob: -discord has no webhook. Tried $HEE_CRED_PASS, then the\n"
            f"sealed '{DISCORD_ACCOUNT}' credential. Seal it once:\n"
            f"  hee cred -seal {DISCORD_ACCOUNT}\n"
            f"or wrap explicitly: hee cred -pass {DISCORD_ACCOUNT} -exec hee scrob -discord"
        )
    body = json.dumps({"content": text}).encode("utf-8")
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        webhook_url, data=body, method="POST",
        headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},
    )
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=10) as resp:
            if resp.status not in (200, 204):
                sys.exit(f"hee-scrob: discord post failed: HTTP {resp.status}")
    except Exception as e:
        sys.exit(f"hee-scrob: discord post failed: {e}")


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="hee-scrob",
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        epilog=(
            f"{PATH_MAP_VAR}: per-oper container->local path map, set in heerc,\n"
            "for when nothing is mounted at Plex's own container path.\n"
            "Comma-separated container=local pairs; longest container prefix wins.\n"
            f'  export {PATH_MAP_VAR}="/data=/srv/media"\n'
        ),
    )
    ap.add_argument("-server", default=None, help=f"the Plex server (default: `server =` in {RC_FILE}, else {DEFAULT_SERVER})")
    ap.add_argument("-players", action="store_true", help="list the players with a session right now and the ones this server knows, marking the claimed ones, and stop")
    ap.add_argument("-claim", metavar="NAME", help=f"claim a player by name or id -- writes one `claim =` line in {RC_FILE} -- and stop")
    ap.add_argument("-unclaim", metavar="NAME", help="drop a claim written by -claim, and stop")
    ap.add_argument("-player", metavar="NAME", help="report this player only, this once, whatever the rc file claims")
    ap.add_argument("-current-only", action="store_true", help="only the exact-moment subtitle line, no before/after context")
    ap.add_argument("-discord", action="store_true", help="post to Discord (webhook URL from $HEE_CRED_PASS) instead of stdout")
    ap.add_argument("-to", metavar="TARGET", choices=["meme"], help="hand what is playing to another tool: `meme` writes a meme-factory quote job for this file and moment (hee meme quote --file --at)")
    ap.add_argument("-job", metavar="NAME", default="np", help="with -to meme: the job's name (default np)")
    # Contextual help BEFORE argparse parses anything. argparse owns -h and
    # --help but not the bare `help` verb, and resolves either only after
    # parsing -- so an unrelated argument errors first and the help never
    # prints. Measured: 8 tools exited non-zero for --help for exactly this
    # reason. See library/py/hee_cli.
    if help_wanted(sys.argv[1:]):
        note_ignored(sys.argv[1:])
        ap.print_help()
        return 0

    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.claim or args.unclaim:
        print(write_claim(args.claim or args.unclaim, remove=bool(args.unclaim)))
        return 0

    rc = read_rc()
    server = args.server or rc["server"] or DEFAULT_SERVER

    token = read_token()
    if not token:
        _store = os.environ.get("HEE_CRED_DIR") or str(Path.home() / ".hee" / "secrets")
        sys.exit(
            "hee-scrob: no token. Tried, in order:\n"
            "  1. $PLEX_TOKEN -- unset\n"
            f"  2. sealed '{CRED_ACCOUNT}' in {_store} -- did not open\n"
            f"  3. {DEFAULT_TOKEN_FILE} -- not present\n"
            f"Seal it once and this tool needs no wrapper afterwards:\n"
            f"  hee cred -seal {CRED_ACCOUNT}\n"
            "If it IS sealed there, this host may hold no secret key for its\n"
            "recipients -- `hee cred -pass " + CRED_ACCOUNT + " -exec true` says which."
        )

    device_names = read_device_names()

    if args.players:
        try:
            playing, known = list_players(server, token, device_names, rc["claims"])
        except (OSError, ET.ParseError, ValueError) as e:
            sys.exit(f"hee-scrob: fetch failed: {e}")
        print(format_players(playing, known, rc["claims"]))
        return 0

    try:
        r = now_playing(server, token, device_names, args.current_only, claims=rc["claims"], want=args.player)
    except Exception as e:
        sys.exit(f"hee-scrob: fetch failed: {e}")

    if not r:
        if args.discord:
            print("hee-scrob: nothing playing, skipping Discord post (silence isn't a message)")
        else:
            print("np: nothing. silence is punk too.")
        return

    line = format_line(r)
    if args.to == "meme":
        print(line)
        return to_meme(r, args.job)
    if args.discord:
        post_discord(line)
    else:
        print(line)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main() or 0)
